KB-1026,辐射安全-辐射工作准入要求,辐射,辐射防护,辐射,4,辐射;放射性,辐射实验室准入与防护要求,进入辐射实验室需要哪些资质和防护要求？,辐射工作人员必须通过环保部门组织的培训，取得《辐射安全与防护培训合格证书》后方可上岗。进行放射性实验前必须由导师进行防护知识培训和安全教育。操作时须采取屏蔽、距离、时间三大防护原则，正确佩戴个人剂量计并接受定期个人剂量监测。购置放射源、射线装置须经学校审批，新建放射性工作场所须经环保部门批准。,取得辐射安全培训合格证;接受导师防护培训;佩戴个人剂量计;严格按SOP操作;定期剂量监测,个人剂量计;防护服;手套;必要时铅围裙,禁止无证上岗;禁止独自操作高风险放射源;禁止在非指定区域操作,放射性废物按国家标准分类存放，交有资质机构处置,发生放射性污染立即疏散，通知辐射安全负责人并就医,放射源丢失或意外照射立即上报学校和环保部门,遵守《放射性同位素与射线装置安全和防护条例》,北京大学医学部实验室安全手册;教育部《高等学校实验室安全规范》,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范（辐射安全部分）,教育部/北京大学医学部,2023,2023-02-21,https://www.cernet.edu.cn/ke_yan_yu_fa_zhan/gai_kuang/xin_wen_gong_gao/202302/t20230221_2301697.shtml,2026-03-15,,draft,辐射;放射性;防护;准入,zh-CN
KB-1027,辐射安全-放射性污染应急处置,辐射,辐射应急,辐射,5,辐射;放射性污染,放射性污染或意外照射,发生放射性污染或意外照射怎么办？,发生放射性同位素丢失、意外照射或放射性污染事故时，应立即疏散周围人员并隔离污染区域，禁止无关人员进入。皮肤污染应立即用流水和肥皂清洗，眼部污染用大量清水冲洗。第一时间通知实验室辐射安全负责人和学校主管部门，根据事故级别逐级上报至生态环境部门。保留事故现场，配合专业人员勘查处置。,疏散人员并隔离区域;皮肤或眼部污染立即冲洗;通知辐射安全负责人;按级别上报环保部门;配合专业机构处置,防护服;手套;个人剂量计;必要时铅围裙,禁止独自处置高风险污染;禁止自行丢弃放射性废物,放射性废物由有资质专业机构统一处置,皮肤污染用流水清洗；内照射或大剂量照射立即就医,立即疏散隔离，逐级上报，启动辐射事故应急预案,遵守《放射性同位素与射线装置安全和防护条例》，须在1小时内上报,北京大学医学部实验室安全手册,应急预案,放射性事故应急处置规范,教育部/高校通用,2023,2023-02-21,https://reagent.bjmu.edu.cn/file/20230414134400495_%E5%AE%9E%E9%AA%8C%E5%AE%A4%E5%AE%89%E5%85%A8%E6%89%8B%E5%86%8C%E5%8D%B0%E5%88%B7%E7%89%8820210610.pdf,2026-03-15,,draft,辐射;放射性污染;应急;事故处置,zh-CN
KB-1028,激光安全-激光器操作规范,电气,激光安全,光学,4,激光;强光辐射,激光器操作与防护,使用激光器时有哪些安全注意事项？,操作激光器时严禁用眼睛直视激光束，包括反射光。不可见的红外激光束尤其危险，须了解光路布局，避免使头部处于激光束水平面。禁止在激光路径上放置易燃物（如黑色纸张、布料、皮革）。操作时摘除所有带反光面的首饰等物品。激光实验室须配备安全屏蔽设施，功率较大的激光器须有互锁装置和防护罩，设置黄色安全警示线。使用前须经过专门培训并留有培训记录。,检查防护设施与互锁装置;清除光路上的易燃物;摘除反光饰物;佩戴对应波长激光护目镜;按SOP开机并确认光路安全,激光专用防护目镜（对应波长）;实验服,禁止直视激光束;禁止在光路附近放易燃物;禁止未培训上岗,损坏的光学元件按一般固废处理,激光伤害眼睛立即用清水冲洗并就医，皮肤灼伤按热灼伤处理,设备故障立即断电并报告实验室管理员,遵守GBZ 2.2激光辐射职业接触限值；实验室接触强度不得超过规定限值,上海交通大学中英国际低碳学院实验室安全与管理规范;北京交通大学实验室安全管理办法,SOP,激光安全操作规范,上海交通大学/高校通用,2021,2021-01-01,https://lcc.sjtu.edu.cn/Assets/userfiles/sys_eb538c1c-65ff-4e82-8e6a-a1ef01127fed/files/sysgl/%E4%B8%8A%E6%B5%B7%E4%BA%A4%E9%80%9A%E5%A4%A7%E5%AD%A6%E4%B8%AD%E8%8B%B1%E5%9B%BD%E9%99%85%E4%BD%8E%E7%A2%B3%E5%AD%A6%E9%99%A2%E5%AE%9E%E9%AA%8C%E5%AE%A4%E5%AE%89%E5%85%A8%E4%B8%8E%E7%AE%A1%E7%90%86%E8%A7%84%E8%8C%83.pdf,2026-03-15,,draft,激光;光学;强光;防护,zh-CN
KB-1029,低温安全-液氮操作规范,化学,低温安全,通用,4,低温;冻伤;窒息,液氮取用与储存操作,使用液氮时需要注意什么？,液氮温度极低（-196摄氏度），可造成严重冻伤，且快速蒸发会使密闭空间严重缺氧。使用前确认房间门窗打开、通风良好；确认皮肤干燥，佩戴绝缘防护手套和防护面罩。往容器注入液氮时动作缓慢，避免飞溅。转移液氮时须使用专用液氮罐，禁止用普通密封容器存放。液氮存储区须安装氧气浓度报警器并定期检测。禁止通过客用电梯运送液氮。,确认通风良好;佩戴绝缘手套和护目镜;缓慢注入液氮;检查氧气报警器状态;安全转移至专用液氮罐,绝缘防护手套;护目镜或防护面罩;实验服,禁止用密封容器存放;禁止在密闭空间大量使用;禁止通过客梯运送;禁止裸手接触,少量液氮可在通风良好处自然蒸发，容器按普通废物处理,冻伤部位用温水（37-40摄氏度）缓慢复温，禁止摩擦，立即就医；缺氧立即转移至通风处,大量泄漏立即疏散，启动应急预案，通知管理人员,遵守实验室特种设备管理规定,宁夏医科大学液氮罐安全使用及管理办法;中国地质大学（北京）液氮操作规程,SOP,液氮安全操作规程,宁夏医科大学/高校通用,2020,2020-12-03,http://www.nxmu.edu.cn/lnjbzdsys/info/1094/1283.htm,2026-03-15,,draft,液氮;低温;冻伤;窒息,zh-CN
KB-1030,机械安全-离心机操作规范,通用,机械安全,通用,3,机械;高速旋转,离心机安全使用,使用离心机有哪些安全规定？,使用离心机时，离心管必须配对平衡，用天平称量确认两侧质量相等；不对称放置可能导致机身剧烈振动乃至损坏。启动前先盖好盖子，从低速开始逐渐调至目标速度。离心过程中禁止打开盖子，必须等到转子完全停止后才能开盖取样。超速会导致离心管破裂，操作前确认所用离心管的最高转速额定值。出现异常振动或噪声立即停机并报告。,天平称量确认平衡;对称放置;盖好盖子;从低速启动;等待完全停止后再开盖,护目镜;实验服,禁止不平衡放置;禁止运转时开盖;禁止超过额定转速,离心管按内容物类型分类处置,离心管破裂造成暴露按相应物质的急救措施处理,异常振动立即切断电源，报告设备管理员检修,遵守实验室仪器设备安全操作规程,上海交通大学生命科学技术学院实验室常见危险设备操作注意事项;中国药科大学实验室安全手册,SOP,离心机安全操作SOP,上海交通大学/高校通用,2023,2023-01-01,https://life.sjtu.edu.cn/En/Data/View/6077,2026-03-15,,draft,离心机;机械;高速旋转;平衡,zh-CN
KB-1031,设备安全-高压灭菌锅操作,生物,高温高压设备,生物,3,高压;高温;蒸汽,高压灭菌器（高压锅）使用,高压灭菌锅使用时需要注意什么？,高压灭菌锅须由经过专项培训的人员操作，全程专人看管，不得擅自离开。操作前检查水位，防止干烧损坏电热管。有压力时联锁手柄不可强制打开，绝对禁止带压开门。液体灭菌结束后须慢速放汽，等液体温度降至安全值后方可开门；开门时操作人员须站在两侧，缓慢开盖，防止残余蒸汽喷出烫伤。灭菌结束后须在内容物上粘贴生物危害标识。,检查水位;放入待灭菌物;设置正确参数;全程看管;放汽降温后开门;贴生物危害标识,耐热手套;护目镜;实验服,禁止带压强制开门;禁止擅自离岗;禁止用于不耐高温物品,灭菌后废弃物按生物危废处理,蒸汽烫伤立即用凉水冷却20分钟，就医,异常升压或泄漏立即断电并疏散，联系维修人员,高压灭菌锅属特种设备，须定期检测,上海交通大学生命科学技术学院实验室常见危险设备操作注意事项,SOP,高压灭菌锅操作SOP,上海交通大学/高校通用,2023,2023-01-01,https://life.sjtu.edu.cn/En/Data/View/6077,2026-03-15,,draft,高压灭菌;灭菌锅;高温;蒸汽,zh-CN
KB-1032,消防-灭火器选择与使用,通用,消防设备,通用,4,火灾;消防,实验室初起火灾处置与灭火器选择,实验室起火了，应该选择哪种灭火器？如何使用？,灭火器选择须根据火灾类型：普通固体火灾（纸、木、布）可用干粉或水；化学试剂或有机溶剂火灾用干粉灭火器；电气火灾须先断电再灭火，使用干粉或二氧化碳灭火器，严禁用水或泡沫灭火器；锂电池火灾用干砂或专用锂电池灭火器，禁止直接用水喷。使用手提式干粉灭火器的步骤：拔出保险销→对准火焰根部→压下手柄→左右扫射。不能控制时立即撤离并拨打119。,判断火灾类型;选择对应灭火器;拔保险销;对准火焰根部;压手柄喷射;无法控制立即撤离报警,避免吸入灭火剂烟雾,禁止向电气火灾用水或泡沫;禁止背对出口扑救;禁止冒险进入浓烟区,使用过的灭火器送维修站检验充装,吸入烟雾不适及时转移至通风处；烧伤用凉水冷却并就医,无法控制立即撤离，拨打119，并到路口引导消防车,遵守《高等学校实验室消防安全管理规范》JY/T 0616—2023,教育部《高等学校实验室消防安全管理规范》JY/T 0616—2023;上海科技大学EHS消防规范,制度,高等学校实验室消防安全管理规范,教育部,JY/T 0616—2023,2023-06-26,https://sysglc.ntu.edu.cn/2024/0326/c8767a230277/page.htm,2026-03-15,,draft,灭火器;消防;火灾;干粉;二氧化碳,zh-CN
KB-1033,应急-火灾疏散逃生规范,通用,应急疏散,通用,4,火灾;疏散,火灾应急疏散,实验室发生火灾时如何正确疏散？,发现火情后立即停止实验，切断电源和气源，组织人员疏散。按照预先熟知的疏散路线（走道、疏散门、安全出口）迅速撤离，不得乘坐电梯。疏散时低姿前行，用湿布捂口鼻减少烟雾吸入。禁止返回烟雾区取财物。撤离后在集合点清点人员，拨打119并报告楼层、起火部位、火势大小。引导消防车进入，不得堵塞消防通道。灭火后实验室须经检查确认安全后方可重新进入。,停止实验;切断电源气源;按疏散图路线撤离;不乘电梯;低姿捂口鼻;集合点清点人员;拨打119,有条件时戴防烟面罩,禁止乘电梯;禁止返回取物;禁止堵塞疏散通道,火灾废弃物须由专业机构处理,吸入浓烟立即转移至空气新鲜处；烧伤立即用凉水冲洗,立即拨打119；保持疏散通道畅通；安全出口不得上锁,遵守《高等学校实验室消防安全管理规范》JY/T 0616—2023；疏散通道不得堵塞,教育部《高等学校实验室消防安全管理规范》JY/T 0616—2023,应急预案,实验室消防应急疏散预案,教育部,JY/T 0616—2023,2023-06-26,https://sysglc.ntu.edu.cn/2024/0326/c8767a230277/page.htm,2026-03-15,,draft,疏散;逃生;消防;应急,zh-CN
KB-1034,生物安全-生物安全柜操作规范,生物,生物安全柜,生物,4,生物污染;气溶胶,生物安全柜内操作感染性材料,生物安全柜如何正确使用？,生物安全柜是防止气溶胶暴露的核心防护设备，使用前须提前开机运行至少15分钟，等待内部气流稳定。操作时前臂缓慢移入，避免破坏气流屏障。柜内物品从清洁区到污染区依次摆放，减少交叉污染。操作感染性材料时穿戴手套、口罩、护目镜和实验服，禁止在柜外进行高风险操作。结束后用70%乙醇擦拭台面，关闭前窗，紫外灯照射仅作为补充消毒措施，不替代其他消毒手段。定期请专业机构检测性能。,提前开机15分钟;检查气流指示;按清洁到污染顺序摆放;操作中不急速进出;结束后乙醇消毒台面;紫外补充消毒,双层手套;口罩或N95;护目镜;实验服,禁止在柜外操作感染性材料;禁止在柜内使用酒精灯明火;禁止用紫外灯代替常规消毒,污染材料按生物危废处理,皮肤暴露立即脱除手套清洗，报告并就医,气溶胶意外释放立即关闭前窗，疏散人员，通知生物安全负责人,遵守《病原微生物实验室生物安全管理条例》,实验室生物安全管理制度;高校生物安全SOP,SOP,生物安全柜操作规程,高校通用/教育部,2021,2021-01-01,https://life.sjtu.edu.cn/En/Data/View/6077,2026-03-15,,draft,生物安全柜;气溶胶;生物安全;BSL,zh-CN
KB-1035,管理-实验室安全分级分类,通用,安全管理,通用,2,管理;综合,了解实验室安全分级分类制度,高校实验室是如何进行安全分级分类管理的？,根据教育部2024年发布的《高等学校实验室安全分级分类管理办法（试行）》，实验室按危险源类别分为：化学类、生物类、辐射类、机电类、其他类。安全等级分为四级：I级（红）重大风险、II级（橙）高风险、III级（黄）中风险、IV级（蓝）低风险。各实验室门外须设安全信息牌，标明安全类别、等级及主要危险源。高风险实验室须安装监控和报警装置，并配备足够的个体防护设备。,按危险源类别判定分类;评估风险等级;设置安全信息牌;安装相应防护设施;落实对应管理措施,根据分级配备相应PPE,禁止降低风险等级标准；禁止不设安全信息牌,,,高风险实验室须制定对应应急预案,依据教育部《高等学校实验室安全分级分类管理办法（试行）》（2024年）,教育部《高等学校实验室安全分级分类管理办法（试行）》2024年4月,制度,高等学校实验室安全分级分类管理办法（试行）,教育部,2024,2024-04-22,https://www.edu.cn/ke_yan_yu_fa_zhan/gai_kuang/xin_wen_gong_gao/202404/t20240422_2600021.shtml,2026-03-15,,draft,分级;分类;管理;教育部;红橙黄蓝,zh-CN
KB-1036,危化品-实验室储存量上限规定,化学,储存规范,化学,4,危化品;易燃;储存,危化品在实验室内的储存量控制,实验室内危化品储存量有上限要求吗？,有严格限制。根据中山大学等高校规定，实验室（除专用储存间外）其余区域暂存危化品总量：液体不超过0.2L每平方米，固体不超过0.2kg每平方米。实验台试剂架仅放当天用量，用完后放回安全柜或试剂柜。气体存储须严格分类：易燃气体不得与助燃气体或剧毒气体同存；氧气不得与油脂混合存放；易燃液体不得与氧化剂混放；氧化剂须单独存放。危化品须存放于专用安全柜，贴有完整标签。,评估当日用量;当日未用完的放回安全柜;气体按类别分区存放;定期盘点并记录,护目镜;实验服;手套,禁止超量储存;禁止易燃气体与助燃气体同存;禁止无标签存放,过期或废弃危化品按危废分类处置,泄漏暴露按对应MSDS急救措施处理,大量泄漏按化学品泄漏应急预案处理,遵守《危险化学品安全管理条例》,中山大学实验室化学品安全管理实施细则;高等学校实验室安全规范,制度,实验室化学品安全管理实施细则,中山大学设备与实验室管理处,2023,2023-01-01,https://sbc.sysu.edu.cn/article/670,2026-03-15,,draft,危化品;储存量;易燃;安全柜,zh-CN
KB-1037,应急-眼部化学灼伤处置,化学,灼伤应急,化学,4,腐蚀性;化学灼伤,化学品溅入眼睛的急救处置,化学品溅入眼睛怎么紧急处理？,化学品溅入眼睛是高优先级紧急情况，必须立即处置。第一步：用大量清水持续冲洗眼睛至少15分钟，冲洗时用手指撑开眼睑，使水流能冲洗眼球各部位；如实验室有洗眼器应立即使用。第二步：冲洗的同时呼叫他人协助，摘除隐形眼镜（若有）。第三步：冲洗后立即就医，并告知医生所接触的化学品种类。禁止揉眼睛，禁止用有色液体中和冲洗。酸、碱或有机溶剂溅入处置原则相同，均以大量清水冲洗为优先。,立即用清水冲洗眼睛15分钟以上;撑开眼睑充分冲洗;摘除隐形眼镜;呼叫他人协助;告知化学品种类后立即就医,操作化学品时佩戴护目镜可预防此类事故,禁止揉搓眼睛;禁止用有色液体冲洗;禁止延误就医,,洗眼器冲洗至少15分钟，立即就医,立即启用洗眼器，呼叫他人协助并拨打急救电话,实验室应配备可用洗眼器，定期检查有效性,高校实验室化学灼伤急救标准操作;GBZ/T 256消毒冲洗规范,应急预案,化学灼伤（眼部）急救规程,高校通用/教育部,2023,2023-01-01,https://sbc.sysu.edu.cn/article/570,2026-03-15,,draft,眼部灼伤;化学灼伤;洗眼器;急救,zh-CN
KB-1038,危化品-丙酮储存与使用,危化品,MSDS,化学,3,易燃;挥发性;麻醉性,丙酮（acetone）的安全使用与储存,丙酮应如何安全储存和使用？,丙酮（CAS 67-64-1）是极易燃液体，沸点56°C，蒸气与空气可形成爆炸性混合物，闪点-18°C。储存于防火安全柜，远离热源、明火和氧化剂，容器密闭并在阴凉通风处存放。使用时必须在通风柜内操作，避免明火和静电；大量使用须做好防静电接地。吸入高浓度蒸气会引起头痛、眩晕，皮肤长期接触可致脱脂。废液收入有机废液桶，不得倒入下水道。,确认通风柜开启;确认无明火和热源;防静电接地;密闭操作;废液入有机废液桶,护目镜;实验服;丁腈手套;通风柜内操作,禁止明火加热;禁止与强氧化剂接触;禁止倒入下水道,有机废液桶，标注（含酮类）,皮肤接触：大量清水冲洗。眼部：大量清水冲洗15分钟，就医。吸入：移至通风处，不适就医,大量泄漏立即切断火源，撤离人员，按化学品泄漏应急预案处理,遵守《危险化学品安全管理条例》,丙酮MSDS（GB/T 16483）;高校实验室危化品管理制度,MSDS,化学品安全数据说明书（SDS）-丙酮,应急管理部化学品登记中心,2023,2023-01-01,https://www.merck.com/products/safety-data-sheets/,2026-03-15,,draft,丙酮;acetone;易燃;有机溶剂,zh-CN
KB-1039,危化品-甲醇储存与使用,危化品,MSDS,化学,4,易燃;有毒;神经毒性,甲醇（methanol）的安全使用与储存,甲醇有什么特别的危险性需要注意？,甲醇（CAS 67-56-1）是高毒易燃液体，对人体神经和视神经有严重损害：少量摄入（10mL）即可导致失明，摄入30mL可致死。蒸气吸入亦有毒性。储存于防火安全柜，远离热源和氧化剂。使用时须在通风柜内操作，严格佩戴PPE。甲醇容易与乙醇混淆，使用前务必确认标签。皮肤吸收也有危险，接触后立即冲洗。废液入有机废液桶并明确标注（甲醇）。,核对标签确认为甲醇;确认通风柜开启;佩戴防护手套;在通风柜内操作;废液专桶收集,护目镜;防渗透手套（丁腈或氯丁橡胶）;实验服;通风柜内操作,禁止明火;禁止误食或吸入蒸气;禁止与乙醇混用不贴标签,有机废液桶，标注（含甲醇/高毒）,皮肤：大量清水冲洗。眼部：冲洗15分钟就医。吸入：移至通风处就医。误食：立即就医（视为中毒急救）,误食立即拨打120，告知甲醇摄入；大量泄漏按应急预案处理,甲醇属高毒物质，需进行双人双锁管理（部分单位要求），遵守《危险化学品安全管理条例》,甲醇MSDS（GB/T 16483）;高校实验室高毒化学品管理,MSDS,化学品安全数据说明书（SDS）-甲醇,应急管理部化学品登记中心,2023,2023-01-01,https://www.merck.com/products/safety-data-sheets/,2026-03-15,,draft,甲醇;methanol;高毒;易燃;失明,zh-CN
KB-1040,危化品-氢氧化钠（NaOH）使用,危化品,MSDS,化学,3,腐蚀性;强碱,NaOH（烧碱/强碱）的安全使用与配制,配制NaOH溶液有什么安全注意事项？,氢氧化钠（NaOH，CAS 1310-73-2）为强腐蚀性固体，溶于水时大量放热。配制溶液时须先加水后缓慢加入NaOH固体，绝不能将水加入NaOH（防止剧烈飞溅）。操作时佩戴防碱护目镜、防化手套和实验服，在通风柜内或开阔通风处进行。NaOH对眼部腐蚀危险尤为严重，接触立即用大量清水冲洗至少15分钟并就医。避免皮肤接触，接触后立即用流水冲洗。储存于密闭容器，防止吸潮和CO2。,佩戴PPE;先加水再加NaOH;缓慢添加并搅拌控温;容器做好标识;废液按碱性废液处理,防碱护目镜或面罩;丁腈手套;实验服,禁止将水加入NaOH;禁止徒手操作;禁止与酸剧烈混合,稀释后按无机碱废液处理，pH调至中性后可按规定排放,皮肤：大量清水冲洗20分钟。眼部：用洗眼器大量清水冲洗至少15分钟，立即就医,大量溅洒启动应急预案，佩戴PPE处理,遵守《危险化学品安全管理条例》,NaOH MSDS（GB/T 16483）;高校实验室化学品安全管理细则,MSDS,化学品安全数据说明书（SDS）-氢氧化钠,应急管理部化学品登记中心,2023,2023-01-01,https://www.merck.com/products/safety-data-sheets/,2026-03-15,,draft,NaOH;氢氧化钠;强碱;腐蚀性;烧碱,zh-CN
KB-1041,危化品-浓硫酸使用,危化品,MSDS,化学,4,腐蚀性;强酸;氧化性;放热,浓硫酸的安全使用与稀释,稀释浓硫酸时为什么要将酸缓慢加入水中，而不是相反？,浓硫酸（H2SO4 >98%，CAS 7664-93-9）稀释时产生大量热，若将水加入浓硫酸，会使溶液局部剧烈沸腾，导致酸液飞溅，造成严重灼伤。正确操作：必须将浓硫酸缓慢地沿容器壁倒入水中（酸入水），同时搅拌散热。操作前确认通风良好，佩戴防酸护目镜和防化手套。浓硫酸有强腐蚀性，接触皮肤应立即用大量清水冲洗（不少于20分钟），眼部接触用洗眼器冲洗后立即就医。储存于阴凉干燥处，远离有机物和还原剂。,佩戴防酸PPE;先在容器中加水;将浓硫酸缓慢沿壁倒入水中;搅拌控温;标识清楚,防酸护目镜或面罩;耐酸手套（厚丁腈或氯丁橡胶）;实验服,禁止将水加入浓硫酸;禁止快速混合;禁止与有机物同存,酸性废液桶；需中和后处理,皮肤：立即大量清水冲洗20分钟。眼部：洗眼器冲洗至少15分钟，立即就医,大量泄漏立即隔离，佩戴防酸PPE用沙土吸附，按应急预案处理,遵守《危险化学品安全管理条例》；浓硫酸属管控化学品，购买需审批,浓硫酸MSDS（GB/T 16483）;配酸SOP;高校化学品安全细则,MSDS,化学品安全数据说明书（SDS）-浓硫酸,应急管理部化学品登记中心,2023,2023-01-01,https://www.merck.com/products/safety-data-sheets/,2026-03-15,,draft,浓硫酸;H2SO4;强酸;腐蚀性;稀释,zh-CN
KB-1042,危化品-氯仿使用,危化品,MSDS,化学,4,有毒;致癌嫌疑;麻醉性;挥发性,氯仿（三氯甲烷）的安全使用,使用氯仿（三氯甲烷）有哪些特殊风险？,氯仿（CHCl3，CAS 67-66-3）是常见有机溶剂，但具有潜在致癌性（IARC 2B类），长期暴露有肝肾损伤风险，吸入高浓度会引起麻醉。所有操作必须在通风柜内进行，不得在柜外开盖、倾倒或转移。氯仿会缓慢氧化生成光气（剧毒），因此须在阴凉避光处储存，避免与铝、镁、强碱接触。接触后皮肤立即清洗，不适立即就医并说明接触的化学品。废液入卤代烃废液桶（不得与非卤代废液混合）。,确认通风柜开启;避光操作;密闭容器;废液入卤代烃废液桶,护目镜;丁腈手套;实验服;通风柜内操作,禁止在柜外操作;禁止与铝/镁/强碱接触;禁止混入非卤代废液,卤代烃废液桶，标注（含氯仿）,皮肤：清水冲洗。眼部：清水冲洗15分钟就医。吸入：移至通风处，有不适立即就医,大量泄漏立即撤离，通风，按应急预案处理,氯仿属IARC 2B类可疑致癌物；遵守危化品管理条例,氯仿MSDS（GB/T 16483）;IARC致癌物分类,MSDS,化学品安全数据说明书（SDS）-氯仿（三氯甲烷）,应急管理部化学品登记中心,2023,2023-01-01,https://www.merck.com/products/safety-data-sheets/,2026-03-15,,draft,氯仿;三氯甲烷;chloroform;致癌;卤代烃,zh-CN
KB-1043,危化品-甲苯储存与使用,危化品,MSDS,化学,3,易燃;挥发性;神经毒性,甲苯（toluene）的安全使用与储存,使用甲苯时需要注意哪些安全事项？,甲苯（CAS 108-88-3）是常见有机溶剂，易燃（闪点4°C），具有神经毒性，长期吸入可损害中枢神经系统，引起头痛、眩晕、疲劳，大剂量暴露可致麻醉。与苯相比，甲苯无已知致癌性，但仍需严格防护。所有操作必须在通风柜内进行，严格佩戴PPE，避免明火和热源（沸点110.6°C，蒸气比空气重，可沿地面扩散至远处火源）。注意甲苯是良好的脂溶性溶剂，可经皮肤吸收，接触后立即冲洗。储存于防火安全柜，远离氧化剂和酸类，容器密闭阴凉存放。废液收入有机废液桶（非卤代）。,确认通风柜开启;确认无明火和热源;防静电操作;密闭操作;废液入有机废液桶,护目镜;丁腈手套;实验服;通风柜内操作,禁止明火加热;禁止在柜外长时间敞口操作;禁止倒入下水道,有机废液桶（非卤代，标注含甲苯）,皮肤接触：大量清水冲洗。眼部：大量清水冲洗15分钟，就医。吸入：移至通风处，不适就医,大量泄漏立即切断火源，撤离人员，通风，按化学品泄漏应急预案处理,遵守《危险化学品安全管理条例》,甲苯MSDS（GB/T 16483）;高校实验室危化品管理制度,MSDS,化学品安全数据说明书（SDS）-甲苯,应急管理部化学品登记中心,2023,2023-01-01,https://www.merck.com/products/safety-data-sheets/,2026-03-15,,draft,甲苯;toluene;易燃;有机溶剂;神经毒性,zh-CN
KB-1044,危险实验开始前最关键的阻断项,通用,开工前检查,通用,4,审批;风险评估;PPE;应急准备,开始高风险实验前的开工判断,危险实验开始前最关键的阻断项是什么？,最关键的阻断项不是某一个单独动作，而是“关键前置条件未满足时一律不得开工”。对高风险实验，至少要同时确认：实验方案和SOP已审核，风险评估已完成，操作者具备培训和授权，所需PPE与通风/联锁/屏蔽条件到位，应急设施和应急联系人可用。只要其中任何一项缺失，都应视为阻断项，先整改后开工。,核对SOP与审批记录;完成风险评估并确认主要危险源;确认人员培训和授权;检查PPE与工程控制是否到位;检查洗眼器/灭火器/应急电话;全部通过后再开始,护目镜;实验服;手套;按场景追加面屏/呼吸防护/绝缘或低温防护,禁止在未审批或未评估状态下开工;禁止缺少PPE仍继续实验;禁止应急设施失效仍强行开展高风险操作,,,发现任一阻断项时立即停止启动并上报实验室负责人,依据《高等学校实验室安全规范》执行高风险实验前置审查与条件确认,高等学校实验室安全规范（教育部2024）;实验室安全分级分类管理办法（试行）,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,开工前检查;阻断项;审批;风险评估;PPE;hazardous experiment;start blocker;pre-start checklist;go/no-go,zh-CN
KB-1045,高风险实验可以一个人单独做吗,通用,人员管理,通用,4,单人作业;高风险;监护,高风险实验人员配置,高风险实验可以一个人单独做吗？,原则上不可以。涉及剧毒、强腐蚀、易燃易爆、高压、高温、低温、辐射、生物暴露、电气带电等高风险实验，通常应实行双人作业或有效监护机制。至少应保证另一名具备响应能力的人员知晓实验内容、能及时求助并协助应急处置。若制度明确要求审批、监护或禁单人作业，应严格执行，不得因“熟练”或“实验时间短”而简化。,识别是否属于高风险实验;查阅本单位SOP与准入要求;落实双人作业或现场监护;确认应急联系人和求助方式;无法满足时延期实验,基础PPE按实验类型配置;高风险场景追加专用防护装备,禁止高风险实验单人夜间进行;禁止无人知情情况下独自操作;禁止以经验替代监护要求,,,若必须中止则先断开危险源并通知负责人重新安排,依据高校实验室安全规范和岗位准入要求落实人员配置,高等学校实验室安全规范（教育部2024）;实验室安全分级分类管理办法（试行）,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,单人作业;高风险实验;监护;准入;working alone;buddy system;high-risk operation;supervision,zh-CN
KB-1046,实验开始前要检查什么,通用,开工前清单,通用,3,清单;SOP;设施;废弃物,实验前常规安全核查,实验开始前要检查什么？,实验开始前建议按“人、物、环境、应急”四类做快速检查。人：确认自己已培训、知道SOP和风险点；物：确认试剂名称、浓度、标签、有效期和设备状态；环境：确认通风、联锁、屏蔽、台面整洁、废弃物容器就位；应急：确认洗眼器、灭火器、出口、应急电话和事故处置卡片可用。对高风险实验，还应加做审批和双人复核。,确认实验目的和SOP;核对试剂标签/浓度/用量;检查设备、通风和联锁;确认PPE穿戴正确;确认废液和危废容器;确认应急设施和联系人,护目镜;实验服;手套;根据实验类型追加专项PPE,禁止标签不清仍使用试剂;禁止设备异常仍启动;禁止未准备废液路径就开始实验,,,发现异常先停用设备或试剂并报告,建议使用标准化开工前检查表并保留记录,高等学校实验室安全规范（教育部2024）;化学化工实验室安全管理规范,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部/高校通用,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,实验前检查;开工前清单;SOP;应急设施;before experiment;pre-start check;startup checklist;pre-lab check,zh-CN
KB-1049,化学品溅入眼睛后的首要处置,化学,事故应急卡片,化学,5,化学暴露;眼部飞溅;应急处置,化学品飞溅进入眼睛后的第一时间处置,化学品溅入眼睛后第一步应该做什么？,第一步不是找老师，也不是先判断药品种类，而是立刻持续冲洗。应立即使用洗眼器或大量流动清水冲洗眼部，持续至少 15 分钟，并尽快通知周围人员协助就医。,立即到洗眼器处持续冲洗;撑开上下眼睑让水流充分接触眼球;如佩戴隐形眼镜尽快取下;通知同伴和实验室负责人;携带化学品标签或 SDS 信息就医,护目镜;面屏;实验服;适配的防化手套,禁止延迟冲洗去查资料;禁止自行点眼药或加中和液;禁止症状稍缓后继续实验,污染纸巾和一次性用品按化学污染废弃物处理,持续冲洗至少 15 分钟后尽快就医,立即通知实验室负责人，必要时启动校内医疗应急流程,洗眼器应处于可用状态并纳入日常点检,高等学校实验室安全规范; 化学品暴露应急处置 SOP,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,化学品入眼;眼睛飞溅;洗眼器;冲洗15分钟;眼部暴露;化学飞溅;eye splash;eyewash,zh-CN
KB-1050,实验室小火何时必须立即撤离,通用,事故应急卡片,通用,5,火灾;撤离;应急处置,实验室初起火灾与立即撤离边界判断,实验室起小火时，什么时候必须立即撤离？,只要火势不再处于可控的小范围初起状态，或疏散路线不清晰，就应立即撤离。若伴随浓烟、未知燃烧物、爆炸风险、气瓶受热或操作者未受过灭火训练，不应继续停留处置。,先警示周围人员;在确保安全的前提下切断气源和电源;确认出口畅通;仅在受训且火势可控时使用正确灭火器;无法迅速控制时立即撤离并报警,撤离优先，不以寻找额外 PPE 为前提,禁止在浓烟中逗留;禁止背对出口灭火;禁止对未知燃烧物随意选用灭火器,灭火残留物按事故污染废弃物处理,热灼伤应冷却处理，吸入烟雾应尽快转移至空气新鲜处并就医,按单位消防预案启动报警和疏散流程,初起火灾处置应建立在培训和演练基础上,高等学校实验室消防安全管理规范; 实验室消防应急预案,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,实验室着火;小火撤离;什么时候撤离;火灾应急;浓烟;爆炸风险;fire evacuation;small lab fire,zh-CN
KB-1051,触电后为什么不能直接用手拉人,通用,事故应急卡片,机电,5,触电;电气事故;应急处置,触电事故的第一时间处置边界,有人触电后为什么不能直接用手把他拉开？,因为在电源未隔离前，伤者和设备可能仍然带电，徒手拉人会让施救者也成为触电者。正确原则是先断电、后接触；无法立刻断电时，也必须使用绝缘物将人员与电源隔离。,优先切断电源或按下急停;无法断电时使用绝缘物隔离;呼叫急救并评估呼吸意识;保护现场并禁止重新送电;事故后进行设备复盘,绝缘手套;绝缘工具;绝缘鞋,禁止未断电徒手拉人;禁止向带电设备泼水;禁止事故后立即重新开机测试,损坏的带电部件按电气故障件管理,断电后按急救流程处理并尽快就医,触电事故应立即升级上报并保留现场,电气事故处置必须遵循先隔离能量再接触伤者的原则,高等学校实验室安全规范; 实验室电气安全 SOP,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,触电;先断电;不能直接拉;绝缘物;电气应急;electric shock;cut power first,zh-CN
KB-1052,未知化学品泄漏时先做什么,化学,事故应急卡片,化学,5,化学泄漏;未知物质;应急处置,未知液体或未知化学品外泄的首要动作,遇到未知化学品泄漏时，第一步是先擦掉吗？,不是。第一步应是停止附近操作并隔离现场，而不是立即上手清理。未知化学品意味着你无法确定其毒性、挥发性和相容性，在风险未识别前，不应贸然擦拭、冲水或移动泄漏物。,停止附近实验和点火源;隔离泄漏区域;识别容器和标签信息;判断是否属于本人训练范围;确认处置路径后再使用吸附和收集工具,护目镜;防化手套;实验服;必要时按 SOP 增加呼吸防护,禁止徒手接触;禁止未确认就直接冲入下水道;禁止在泄漏附近继续加热或明火操作,污染吸附材料和清理工具按危废收集,如已暴露人体，按暴露部位执行冲洗和就医流程,大面积、强挥发或身份未知的泄漏应立即升级处置,未知化学品按高风险泄漏事件处理,高等学校实验室安全规范; 化学品泄漏应急 SOP,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,未知泄漏;未知化学品;先隔离;不要先擦;spill isolation;unknown chemical,zh-CN
KB-1053,高风险实验审批重点审查内容,通用,高风险审批,通用,4,审批;风险评估;管理,高风险实验审批的核心审查项,高风险实验审批一般重点审查哪些内容？,高风险审批不是只看导师是否同意，而是审查整套前置条件是否完整。重点通常包括实验目的与步骤是否明确、危险源识别是否充分、风险评估是否完成、控制措施和 PPE 是否匹配、操作者是否受训授权、是否需要双人作业，以及应急预案和废弃物路径是否清晰。,提交实验方案和 SOP;补充风险评估表;明确控制措施与应急方案;核验人员资质和准入;审批通过后再排期执行,在审批阶段就应明确 PPE 标准，而不是临场决定,禁止无风险评估先审批;禁止用口头同意替代正式审查;禁止审批后擅自扩大实验边界,审批阶段应同时确认废液和危险废物去向,审批表中应包含主要事故情形的应急要点,审批未完成前不得进入开工状态,高风险实验应留痕管理并落实前置审查,高等学校实验室安全规范; 实验室安全分级分类管理办法,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,高风险审批;审批审什么;审批重点;风险评估;前置审查;approval checklist;hazard review,zh-CN
KB-1054,审批通过后为什么还要双人复核,通用,高风险审批,通用,4,审批;双人复核;监护,审批与现场复核的关系,高风险实验审批通过以后，还需要双人复核吗？,通常仍然需要。审批解决的是计划层面是否允许开展，双人复核解决的是现场条件此刻是否真正满足。很多事故并不是没有审批，而是现场执行时出现标签错误、联锁未开、PPE 不合规、人员状态不适合等问题，所以审批不能替代现场双人复核。,按审批意见准备实验;由第二人复核关键项;确认设备、试剂、PPE 和应急设施状态;全部通过后再启动,按审批要求逐项落实，不得临时削减,禁止以审批通过为理由跳过现场复核;禁止单人自行确认全部关键项,如复核发现废弃物路径不清，应先整改再开工,复核阶段应再次确认事故处置资源可用,发现关键项异常时应立即暂停开工并回退整改,高风险实验应同时满足书面审批和现场条件确认,高等学校实验室安全规范; 实验室安全分级分类管理办法,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,双人复核;审批通过后;还要复核吗;buddy check;现场复核;secondary review,zh-CN
KB-1055,临时改步骤改试剂是否需要重新审批,通用,高风险审批,通用,4,变更管理;审批;风险重评,高风险实验执行中的临时变更,实验过程中如果临时改步骤、改试剂或改设备，需要重新审批吗？,如果变更会影响危险源、风险等级、控制措施、操作边界或废弃物路径，就应视为实质性变更，原则上需要重新评估并重新审批。很多事故正是由未经评估的小改动触发的，所以不能把“只改一点点”当成跳过审批的理由。,先暂停执行;比较变更前后的危险源和控制条件;如影响风险边界则重新评估并提交审批;批准后再继续,变更后同步检查 PPE 是否仍然适配,禁止边做边改且不留痕;禁止实质性变更不重新评估;禁止口头通知代替正式变更记录,变更后若产生新型废物，应重新确认分类和去向,变更后应同步更新可能事故类型与急救要点,临时变更导致风险不确定时，应回退到安全状态,高风险实验应实施变更管理和再评估,高等学校实验室安全规范; 实验室安全分级分类管理办法,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,改步骤;改试剂;改设备;重新审批;变更管理;change control;风险重评;procedure change,zh-CN
KB-1056,夜间或节假日能否开展高风险实验,通用,高风险审批,通用,4,夜间实验;节假日实验;审批,夜间和节假日开展高风险实验的边界,夜间或者节假日可以安排高风险实验吗？,原则上应从严控制。只有在审批明确允许、双人值守、应急资源可用、导师或管理链路在线、现场条件稳定的情况下，才可能安排。若任何一项前置条件不足，就不应在夜间或节假日开展高风险实验。,核查是否属于必须安排的任务;确认审批意见覆盖夜间或节假日场景;落实双人值守和通讯链路;确认应急物资和保卫值班可用;条件不满足则改期,与日间标准一致，不因时段降低要求,禁止以赶进度为由默认夜间开工;禁止单人夜间开展高风险实验;禁止无值班支撑时强行开展,夜间结束后不得将危险废物随意滞留于无管理状态,应确认夜间医疗和应急联络方式可达,通讯链路、值班或门禁异常时应立即取消计划,夜间和节假日实验需满足更严格的审批和监护要求,高等学校实验室安全规范; 实验室值班与高风险实验管理制度,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,夜间实验;节假日实验;能不能做;高风险实验夜间;after-hours;working alone at night,zh-CN
KB-1057,培训未通过能否独立开展高风险实验,通用,培训考核,通用,4,培训;准入;授权,培训成绩与高风险实验准入之间的关系,实验室培训没有通过，还能独立开展高风险实验吗？,不能。培训是否通过直接关联准入资格。未通过培训说明对 SOP、风险边界、应急处置或禁止事项掌握不足，此时不应获得独立开展高风险实验的授权，应先补训、复测并通过后再进入独立操作阶段。,暂停独立上机或独立开工资格;查看错题和薄弱类别;完成补训和复测;通过后再恢复授权,培训未通过并不会降低现场风险，因此 PPE 要求不能打折,禁止培训未通过人员独立开展高风险实验;禁止以口头承诺代替考核通过;禁止代答代考,培训记录和准入记录应同步留档,补训内容应覆盖主要事故类型的急救流程,若发现未授权人员已在独立操作，应立即叫停并上报,培训考核结果应与岗位授权和设备准入联动,高等学校实验室安全规范; 实验室人员准入与培训制度,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,培训没通过;不能独立实验;高风险准入;补训;training pass;authorization;独立操作,zh-CN
KB-1058,新成员首次进实验室前要完成哪些培训,通用,培训考核,通用,3,培训;新成员;准入,新成员入室前的基础培训清单,新成员第一次进入实验室前，一般要先完成哪些培训？,至少应完成实验室通用安全教育、所在实验室 SOP 学习、危险源与 PPE 基础、废弃物分类、应急设施位置与报警流程，以及与本人任务相关的专项培训。进入高风险区域前，还应完成设备上岗或专项授权培训。,完成通用安全教育;学习本实验室规章和 SOP;熟悉危险源、PPE 与废弃物要求;识别应急设施和疏散路线;需要时完成专项设备培训,培训中应明确不同任务对应的 PPE 基线要求,禁止未经培训直接入室操作;禁止只看视频不做现场确认;禁止将签到视为培训完成,培训中应包含危废暂存和交接流程,入室前应掌握基础急救和报警方式,新成员应能独立指出洗眼器、灭火器和出口位置,新成员培训应与准入记录绑定留痕,高等学校实验室安全规范; 实验室新成员准入制度,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,新成员培训;首次进实验室;入门培训;专项培训;onboarding;lab induction;准入培训,zh-CN
KB-1059,错题本和培训记录除了留档还有什么用,通用,培训考核,通用,2,培训;错题复盘;持续改进,培训记录在持续改进中的作用,实验室培训里的错题本和培训记录，除了留档还有什么意义？,它们的价值不只是证明“培训做过了”，更重要的是发现谁在哪些类别上反复出错，从而安排定向补训、调整题库、优化 SOP 表达，并把培训结果真正接入准入管理。错题本是风险画像，培训记录是持续改进依据。,统计错题类别;识别高频薄弱点;安排定向补训;修订题库和操作提示;将结果反馈到准入与监督机制,若某类 PPE 相关题目频繁出错，应增加现场示教,禁止把培训记录当成纯行政台账;禁止只记录分数不看错题分布,不涉及,高频错题若涉及应急，应优先补训,连续错在高风险题目上时，应临时限制相关操作权限,培训档案应服务于能力改进而非只为留痕,高等学校实验室安全规范; 实验室培训与能力评估制度,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,错题本;培训记录;除了留档;持续改进;mistake review;training log;补训,zh-CN
KB-1060,培训通过率持续偏低时管理员先做什么,通用,培训考核,通用,3,培训管理;统计分析;持续改进,培训通过率低时的管理动作,如果实验室培训通过率一直偏低，管理员应该先做什么？,第一步不是简单提高难度或重复考试，而是先看错题分布和薄弱类别，判断是题目表述问题、培训内容不到位、现场规则和题库不一致，还是新成员结构变化导致。只有先定位原因，后续补训和制度修订才有效。,拉取通过率和错题类别统计;定位薄弱知识点;核对题库与现行 SOP 是否一致;组织针对性补训;复测后再看是否改善,若 PPE 类题目错误集中，应增加实操检查环节,禁止只看总通过率不看原因;禁止长期使用过期题库;禁止培训与现场规则脱节,不涉及,若急救类题目通过率低，应优先补齐,连续两轮低通过率时应触发专项整改,培训通过率应与整改机制和看板联动,高等学校实验室安全规范; 实验室培训与能力评估制度,制度,高等学校实验室安全规范,教育部,2024,2024-09-12,https://www.moe.gov.cn/srcsite/A16/moe_784/202302/t20230220_1045998.html,2026-03-31,,draft,培训通过率低;管理员先做什么;错题分析;题库复核;training pass rate;weak category,zh-CN
WEBX-001-001,Chemical Hygiene Plan,化学,安全管理,化学,4,危化品,How should a laboratory Chemical Hygiene Plan address particularly hazardous substances and high-hazard operations,How should a laboratory Chemical Hygiene Plan address particularly hazardous substances and high-hazard operations有哪些要点？,"# Chemical Hygiene Plan Print this page The Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulation [29 CFR 1910.1450, Occupational Exposure to Hazardous Chemicals in Laboratories](https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.1450), requires employers to develop and implement a Chemical Hygiene Plan (CHP) that protects laboratory personnel from chemical health and safety hazards and ensures exposures remain below applicable OSHA Permissible Exposure Limits (PELs). This document constitutes the official Cornell University Chemical Hygiene Plan. Environment, Health and Safety (EHS) maintains the institutional CHP and provides program oversight, technical guidance, and supporting resources. The CHP establishes the minimum safety requirements for the use, handling, and management of hazardous chemicals in all Cornell research and teaching laboratories. Laboratories and departments may develop additional manuals, procedures, and standard operating procedures (SOPs) to address local hazards or specialized operations. These documents must supplement—and cannot replace or reduce—the requirements of the Cornell University Chemical Hygiene Plan. **As required under the OSHA Laboratory Standard, the CHP includes the following elements:** ## Standard Operating Procedures Laboratories using hazardous chemicals must maintain written standard operating procedures (SOPs) that describe the safe handling, storage, and disposal practices for the work performed. SOPs must be readily accessible to laboratory personnel and incorporated into laboratory-specific training. Many departments maintain laboratory safety manuals or procedure sets; these documents supplement—but do not replace— the requirement for SOPs specific to the chemicals, equipment, and processes used within each laboratory. EHS provides guidance, templates, and technical support to assist laboratories in developing both general and chemical- or process-specific SOPs. Principal Investigators and laboratory supervisors are responsible for ensuring that: * **SOPs exist** for all operations involving hazardous chemicals, including work with particularly hazardous substances (PHS), such as select carcinogens, reproductive toxins, acute toxins, and highly reactive or unstable materials. * **Engineering controls, administrative controls, and personal protective equipment** identified in SOPs are appropriate for the hazards and implemented in daily practice. * **SOPs are reviewed and updated** when procedures, hazards, equipment, or personnel change, and as part of routine laboratory safety reviews. SOPs must evolve with research activities and reflect current hazards, control measures, and best practices. For assistance with SOP development or review, contact [askEHS@cornell.edu](mailto:askEHS@cornell.edu?subject=Lab%20Safety%20%E2%80%93%20SOP%20Development%20Assistance). ## Control Measures Laboratories must implement engineering controls, administrative controls, safe work practices, and personal protective equipment (PPE) to minimize exposures to hazardous chemicals, in accordance with the OSHA Laboratory Standard and the Cornell University Chemical Hygiene Plan (CHP). These requirements complement the laboratory safety practices outlined in the Cornell Laboratory Safety Manual and, when applicable, the Biosafety Written Program and Radiation Safety Manual. ### Engineering Controls Engineering controls are the primary means of minimizing exposure to hazardous chemicals. Examples include chemical fume hoods, local exhaust ventilation systems, ventilated chemical storage, snorkel hoods, biosafety cabinets (when appropriate for chemical compatibility), and other systems designed to reduce airborne exposures. * Chemical fume hoods must be used whenever feasible, particularly for volatile, flammable, corrosive, toxic, reactive, or unknown chemicals. * Hood sashes must remain at or below the indicated safe operating height. * Work may not be conducted in a hood that is out of service, undergoing repair, or displaying a failed certification tag. * Ventilation alarms and airflow monitors must not be overridden or ignored. ### General Laboratory Practices EHS provides guidance on general laboratory work practices based on _Prudent Practices in the Laboratory_ and the Cornell Laboratory Safety Manual. These practices include safe chemical handling, housekeeping, spill prevention, segregation of incompatible materials, proper use of ventilation systems, and minimizing the scale of hazardous operations. Where chemical hazards intersect with biological or radiological materials, applicable procedures in the Biosafety Written Program and Radiation Safety Manual must also be followed. ### Specific Laboratory Practices Laboratories must develop written procedures when unique hazards or processes require more detailed controls. Operations involving particularly hazardous substances (PHS)—including select carcinogens, reproductive toxins, acute toxins, pyrophorics, and highly reactive materials—must incorporate designated areas, enhanced engineering controls, and clearly defined SOPs consistent with the CHP. EHS can assist with hazard evaluations and the development or review of SOPs for high-hazard operations. Contact [askEHS@cornell.edu](mailto:askEHS@cornell.edu?subject=Lab%20Safety%20%E2%80%93%20Hazard-Specific%20SOP%20Assistance). ### Laboratory Attire Laboratory-appropriate attire is required at all times in spaces where hazardous chemicals are used or stored. At a minimum, clothing must fully cover the torso, legs, and ankles, and shoes must be closed-toe and closed-heel. Clothing should be made of materials that provide suitable protection against incidental chemical contact. ### Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) When hazardous chemicals are present, the minimum required personal protective equipment consists of: * safety glasses with side protection (or goggles when splash hazards are present), * a laboratory coat appropriate for the work performed, and * protective gloves worn whenever handling hazardous chemicals or contaminated materials. Additional PPE—such as chemical-splash goggles, face shields, chemical-resistant aprons, flame-resistant lab coats, aprons, or respiratory protection—may be required depending on the hazards and procedures involved. Respirator use must comply with Cornell’s Respiratory Protection Program, including medical clearance, fit testing, and training. For assistance with PPE selection, contact [askEHS@cornell.edu](mailto:askEHS@cornell.edu?subject=Lab%20Safety%20%E2%80%93%20PPE%20Selection%20Guidance). ### Other Controls Additional control measures may include exposure monitoring (when warranted), inspection and verification of emergency equipment, selection of compatible and appropriately rated chemical containers, and substitution of less hazardous chemicals or processes whenever feasible. ## Fume Hoods and Other Protective Equipment Chemical fume hoods are tested annually by Facilities and Campus Services (FCS) to verify airflow performance and safe operation. Emergency eyewash units and emergency showers are tested annually by Environment, Health and Safety (EHS) to ensure proper function and compliance with applicable safety standards. Laboratories are responsible for performing weekly activation of plumbed emergency eyewashes to ensure clear flow and readiness for use. If any fume hood, eyewash, or emergency shower is found to be nonfunctional, obstructed, or otherwise unable to provide adequate protection, laboratory personnel must cease work that depends on the equipment and report the issue immediately. Additional protective systems—including fire extinguishers, mechanical ventilation systems, biosafety cabinets, radiation shielding, and spill response equipment—are maintained by FCS, EHS, or other designated service groups. Detailed requirements for specialized containment devices are described in the Cornell Laboratory Safety Manual, Biosafety Written Program, and Radiation Safety Manual. For non-emergency questions or to report equipment concerns, contact [askEHS@cornell.edu](mailto:askEHS@cornell.edu?subject=Lab%20Safety%20%E2%80%93%20Protective%20Equipment%20Concern) or call EHS at 607-255-8200. ## Information and Training All laboratory personnel must receive information and training as required under OSHA regulations and Cornell University Policy. Required training includes Laboratory Safety and Chemical Waste Disposal, with additional training assigned based on laboratory-specific hazards, processes, or regulatory program requirements. Training is delivered through EHS and documented in the University’s learning management system. Principal Investigators and laboratory supervisors are responsible for ensuring that laboratory personnel: * complete all required EHS training prior to performing independent laboratory work, * receive **laboratory-specific training** covering the hazards present in their work area, applicable SOPs, emergency procedures, and required protective measures, * receive **process- or task-specific training** when working with high-hazard chemicals, specialized equipment, or operations requiring enhanced controls, * are provided training that aligns with other applicable Cornell safety programs, including the Laboratory Safety Manual, Biosafety Written Program, Radiation Safety Manual, and Laser Safety Program, when these hazards intersect with chemical use, * have ready access to Safety Data Sheets (SDSs), laboratory SOPs, and other relevant hazard information. SDSs must be readily accessible to all laboratory personnel at all times. EHS maintains resources for SDS access and provides assistance upon request at [askEHS@cornell.edu](mailto:askEHS@cornell.edu?subject=Lab%20Safety%20%E2%80%93%20SDS%20Access%20Support). For non-emergency questions or training assistance, contact EHS at 607-255-8200 or email [askEHS@cornell.edu](mailto:askEHS@cornell.edu?subject=Lab%20Safety%20%E2%80%93%20Training%20or%20Information%20Request). For emergencies, dial **911** from a Cornell landline or **607-255-1111** from a mobile phone. ## Prior Approval for High-Hazard Work Certain laboratory operations involve elevated risks due to the chemical, physical, or procedural hazards involved. Examples include work with pyrophoric or water-reactive materials, hydrofluoric acid, acutely toxic gases, highly reactive chemicals, peroxide-forming compounds, or processes involving significant heat, pressure, or scale-up. These activities may require additional review before work begins to ensure appropriate controls are in place. Because research practices vary widely across the University, a single institution-wide prior approval process is not feasible. Instead, **Principal Investigators and laboratory supervisors are responsible for identifying high-hazard operations in their laboratories and ensuring that appropriate review and control measures are documented in laboratory SOPs or other internal procedures.** EHS is available to assist in identifying high-hazard conditions, reviewing associated controls, and providing guidance on work involving particularly hazardous substances (PHS), such as select carcinogens, reproductive toxins, and acute toxins. Additional resources, including SDSs and reference materials such as _Prudent Practices in the Laboratory_ and _Safety in Academic Chemistry Laboratories_ , may also inform laboratory-specific review processes. When high-hazard chemical work intersects with biological or radiological hazards or specialized containment systems, the requirements of the Laboratory Safety Manual, Biosafety Written Program, and Radiation Safety Manual also apply. ## Medical Consultations and Medical Examinations Medical consultations and medical examinations will be made available to laboratory personnel as required under OSHA regulations. These evaluations are provided at no cost to the employee, at a reasonable time and place, and are performed by or under the supervision of a licensed physician through Cornell University Occupational Medicine. Laboratory personnel must be provided the opportunity for a medical consultation whenever there is a concern that exposure to a hazardous chemical may have occurred. Medical evaluations will be provided when: * an employee develops signs or symptoms associated with exposure to a hazardous chemical used in the laboratory, * exposure monitoring indicates an exposure level above an OSHA action level or Permissible Exposure Limit (PEL), or * a spill, leak, explosion, or similar incident may have resulted in a potential exposure. Medical records associated with consultations, examinations, and physician opinions are maintained by Cornell University Occupational Medicine in accordance with [29 CFR 1910.1020](https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.1020). Exposure monitoring records are maintained by EHS. For exposure-related concerns, incident follow-up, or guidance on next steps, contact EHS at 607-255-8200 or email [askEHS@cornell.edu](mailto:askEHS@cornell.edu?subject=Lab%20Safety%20%E2%80%93%20Exposure%20Follow-Up). ## Personnel Responsible for the Chemical Hygiene Plan Environment, Health and Safety (EHS) maintains the institutional Chemical Hygiene Plan (CHP) and provides program oversight, technical guidance, training, and support for chemical safety across Cornell University laboratories. As required under OSHA’s Laboratory Standard (29 CFR 1910.1450), Cornell designates a Chemical Hygiene Officer (CHO) within EHS to develop, implement, and maintain the CHP and to advise departments and laboratories on compliance and best practices. ### Chemical Hygiene Officer (CHO) As required under OSHA’s Laboratory Standard (29 CFR 1910.1450), Cornell University designates a Chemical Hygiene Officer (CHO) within Environment, Health and Safety (EHS). The CHO is responsible for developing, implementing, and maintaining the Chemical Hygiene Plan and for providing technical guidance in the implementation of the CHP across all Cornell research and teaching laboratories. The CHO’s responsibilities include: * maintaining and updating the Chemical Hygiene Plan; * providing expert guidance on chemical hazard evaluation, control measures, and laboratory practices; * advising Principal Investigators and laboratory supervisors on compliance with the OSHA Laboratory Standard and Cornell policies; * coordinating with Facilities and Campus Services, departmental safety representatives, and other EHS programs to ensure alignment of chemical safety practices; * assisting in the development of SOPs and laboratory hazard assessments when requested; * reviewing trends in laboratory incidents, inspections, and near-misses to support continuous improvement in chemical safety. While the CHO provides technical expertise and institutional oversight, responsibility for implementing the CHP within individual laboratories rests with Principal Investigators and laboratory supervisors, as required by Cornell University Policy 8.6. Principal Investigators, laboratory supervisors, departments, and colleges are responsible for ensuring that work conducted in their laboratories complies with the requirements of the CHP and that appropriate engineering controls, administrative controls, PPE, and laboratory-specific procedures are in place. Local procedures or supplemental laboratory or departmental chemical hygiene plans may be developed, provided they meet or exceed the requirements of the OSHA Laboratory Standard and the Cornell University CHP. The CHO and EHS staff are available to assist with hazard evaluations, SOP development, laboratory practices, training, and interpretation of regulatory requirements. For assistance, contact EHS at 607-255-8200 or email [askEHS@cornell.edu](mailto:askEHS@cornell.edu?subject=Lab%20Safety%20%E2%80%93%20CHP%20Support). ## Provisions for Additional Protection When Working with Particularly Hazardous Substances The OSHA Laboratory Standard requires additional protective measures for work involving particularly hazardous substances (PHS), including select carcinogens, reproductive toxins, and acutely toxic chemicals. Similar elevated controls are also required for other high-hazard materials such as pyrophoric, water-reactive, or highly reactive chemicals, and for procedures that present an increased risk of fire, explosion, or uncontrolled release. Principal Investigators and laboratory supervisors are responsible for identifying PHS and other high-hazard operations in their laboratories and ensuring that appropriate controls are implemented and documented in laboratory SOPs and training. EHS is available to assist in evaluating hazards and recommending additional safeguards to protect laboratory personnel. Additional protective measures may include: * establishing designated areas for PHS or high-hazard chemical use, * using containment devices such as fume hoods, gloveboxes, or other appropriate engineering controls, * implementing procedures for the safe handling, storage, and removal of contaminated materials and waste, * enhanced decontamination procedures for work surfaces and equipment, and * incorporating these requirements into laboratory-specific SOPs, hazard assessments, and training. When PHS or high-hazard chemical use intersects with biological or radiological hazards or involves specialized containment systems, the requirements of the Laboratory Safety Manual, Biosafety Written Program, and Radiation Safety Manual also apply. For assistance with evaluating PHS or developing required controls, contact EHS at 607-255-8200 or email [askEHS@cornell.edu](mailto:askEHS@cornell.edu?subject=Lab%20Safety%20%E2%80%93%20PHS%20Support). _This Chemical Hygiene Plan is reviewed annually. Last Date Reviewed: 11/2025._",,,,,,,基于公开网页资料自动抽取，导入知识库前请人工复核。提取通道=direct；quality_score=0.741。,Chemical Hygiene Plan | https://ehs.cornell.edu/research-safety/chemical-safety/chemical-hygiene-plan,网页,Chemical Hygiene Plan,Cornell University EHS,,,https://ehs.cornell.edu/research-safety/chemical-safety/chemical-hygiene-plan,2026-04-27,,draft,chemical hygiene plan;PHS;SOP;training;EHS,en
WEBX-002-001,Chemical Hygiene Plan,化学,安全管理,化学,4,危化品;化学;综合安全,What responsibilities and components are required in a laboratory Chemical Hygiene Plan,What responsibilities and components are required in a laboratory Chemical Hygiene Plan有哪些要点？,"Skip to main content # Chemical Hygiene Plan ## Chemical Hygiene Plan - _Online Component_ Cal/OSHA ([Title 8 CCR Sec. 5191](http://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/5191.html)) requires that all laboratories have a written Chemical Hygiene Plan as a fundamental chemical safety plan for the laboratory. This online resource is intended to supplement the information in the hard copy Chemical Hygiene Plan (CHP) Flipchart that includes laboratory-specific information. ### Request a CHP Flipchart To obtain a CHP Flip Chart for your laboratory, use the**[contact form](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/about#contact)** and select the ""_**Safety signage, stickers, flipcharts, or other materials**_ "" option. You can also call (510) 642-3073 or email [ucbcho@berkeley.edu](mailto:ucbcho@berkeley.edu). ## Chemical Hygiene Responsibilities All UC Berkeley laboratory personnel who work with laboratory chemicals must know and follow the guidance outlined in this online CHP Component and the CHP Flipchart. Every laboratory worker is responsible for following safe work practices. Practicing safe chemical hygiene protects you and other lab members. Additional requirements for this laboratory may also be found in the lab’s [Radiation Use Authorization](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/safety-subjects/radiation-safety/radiation-use-authorizations), [Biological Use Authorization](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/biological-safety), [Laser User Registration](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/laser-safety), or if you use [Toxic Gases](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/publications/uc-berkeley-toxic-corrosive-and-pyrophoric-tcp-gas-program-document) or [Controlled Substances](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/controlled-substances). Contact Campus Chemical Hygiene Officer, Kelsey Mesa, at [ucbcho@berkeley.edu](mailto:ucbcho@berkeley.edu) or (510) 642-3073 with any questions regarding the CHP or any lab safety issues. ## Table of Contents [Hazardous Chemicals/Materials](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/chemical-hygiene-plan/hazardous-chemicalsmaterials) [Controlling Exposure](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/chemical-hygiene-plan/controlling-exposures) [Required Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/chemical-hygiene-plan/required-personal-protective-equipment-ppe) [Standard Operating Procedures](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/chemical-hygiene-plan/standard-operating-procedure-sop-requirements) [Campus Policies](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/chemical-hygiene-plan/campus-policies) [Medical Consultation](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/chemical-hygiene-plan/medical-consultation) [Activities Requiring Prior Approval](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/chemical-hygiene-plan/activities-requiring-prior-approval) [Training](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/chemical-hygiene-plan/training-requirements) ## Topics [Lab Safety Manual topic page](/topics/lab-safety-manual)",,,,,,,基于公开网页资料自动抽取，导入知识库前请人工复核。提取通道=direct；quality_score=0.5587。,Chemical Hygiene Plan | https://ehs.berkeley.edu/safety-subjects/chemical-safety/chemical-hygiene-plan,网页,Chemical Hygiene Plan,UC Berkeley EH&S,,,https://ehs.berkeley.edu/safety-subjects/chemical-safety/chemical-hygiene-plan,2026-04-27,,draft,chemical hygiene plan;Cal OSHA;CHP;lab responsibilities,en
WEBX-003-001,Information on Hydrofluoric Acid,化学,化学品管理,化学,5,危化品;剧毒;综合安全,What controls and emergency actions are required when using hydrofluoric acid in laboratories,What controls and emergency actions are required when using hydrofluoric acid in laboratories有哪些要点？,"[__Print](javascript:window.print\(\)) [](https://www.addtoany.com/add_to/email?linkurl=https%3A%2F%2Fehs.stanford.edu%2Freference%2Finformation-hydrofluoric-acid&linkname=Information%20on%20Hydrofluoric%20Acid ""Email"")[](https://www.addtoany.com/add_to/copy_link?linkurl=https%3A%2F%2Fehs.stanford.edu%2Freference%2Finformation-hydrofluoric-acid&linkname=Information%20on%20Hydrofluoric%20Acid ""Copy Link"") Hydrofluoric acid, a solution of hydrogen fluoride gas (HF) in water, is one of the most corrosive and dangerous chemicals encountered in the laboratory. Exposure to HF can cause severe tissue damage and even death. The fluoride ions in HF readily bind calcium and magnesium, reducing their levels in the blood, resulting in hypotension and cardiac effects.1 Deaths have been reported from concentrated acid burns (involving ≥ 50% HF solutions) to as little as 2.5% of body surface area, which is equivalent to approximately one hand and forearm.2 In lower concentrations, symptoms may be delayed, which can lead to delayed recognition of exposure and a more severe burn. The following special safety precautions are necessary when using this chemical, regardless if using dilute or concentrated HF. ### What are the hazards? Hydrofluoric acid (HF) exposure is very toxic and can be fatal if not treated immediately. HF is absorbed quickly; however, damage/symptoms can occur hours to days later. **Any person exposed to HF must have _immediate_ first aid, followed by _immediate_ medical treatment from a physician.** When seeking medical attention bring a copy of this HF Fact Sheet, the Safety Data Sheet (SDS) for the product used, and the [Honeywell guide, “Recommended Medical Treatment for Hydrofluoric Acid Exposure](https://www.honeywell-hfacid.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/06/2734-Medical-Treatment-for-HF-Acid-Exposure_v7-WEB.pdf)” to the Stanford Hospital Emergency Room. ### What are the symptoms of exposure? ##### INHALATION3 * Mild effects include irritation, inflammation, and cough. * Severe effects include narrowing and swelling of the throat, causing upper airway obstruction. * Effects may appear immediately or evolve over 12-36 hours. ##### Skin/eye exposure3 * Exposure to concentrations >50% HF cause immediate and severe painful, throbbing burns with whitish discoloration of the skin. Blisters often form. * Exposure to concentrations between 20 and 50% may produce pain and swelling, but may not be felt for up to 8 hours following exposure. * Exposure to concentrations <20% may cause no immediate pain, but serious injury can develop over 12-24 hours. * Eye exposure can cause destruction or opacification of the cornea, which can result in blindness. * Pulmonary (lung) effects can result from skin and eye exposures. ### How can I protect myself? ##### ENGINEERING CONTROLS * Work in a fume hood with the sash opened as little as possible — sash must not be opened beyond the stickered arrow. * Ensure the nearest emergency safety shower / eyewash is accessible and has been tested in the last month. * Ensure laboratory fume hood has been certified within the last 12 months and is functioning properly (check sticker on fume hood and inward airflow monitor). ##### **WORK PRACTICES** * Purchase and use the smallest quantities and lowest concentrations necessary. * Do not submerge hands into HF solutions. Use tweezers, cassettes, and other sample holders. Teflon and polypropylene are compatible materials. * Establish designated area for HF use and post sign “Hydrofluoric Acid Use Area.” Also post sign on lab door when in use. * Do not work alone; others present in the laboratory must be familiar with the operation’s hazards and emergency procedures. * Add acid to water, not water to acid. * Do not use glass, ceramic, or other incompatible containers with HF. * Ensure secondary containment and segregation of incompatible compatible chemicals; see SU [Compatible Storage Group Classification System](https://ehs.stanford.edu/forms-tools/stanford-storage-groups). * Store HF solutions below eye level. ##### **PERSONAL PROTECTIVE EQUIPMENT (PPE)** * Check PPE for damage before using. * Wear appropriate PPE, which minimally includes: * Goggles and face shield. * Butyl rubber, neoprene, or MAPA trionic tripolymer gloves. Verify glove selection based on concentration and potential exposure via SU’s [Laboratory Chemical Glove Selection Guidance](https://ehs.stanford.edu/forms-tools/laboratory-chemical-glove-selection-guidance). * Neoprene long-sleeve apron if splash/spray is possible. * Lab coat and closed-toe shoes. ##### STANDARD OPERATING PROCEDURE (SOP) * Develop an SOP, which is a set of written instructions that describes in detail how to perform a laboratory process or experiment safely and effectively. * Review the guidance for prioritizing [SOP development](https://ehs.stanford.edu/manual/chemical-hygiene-plan/sop-development). * Plan the operation to eliminate risk of HF splash/ spray. * Perform a “dry run” of procedure using non-hazardous materials to identify and correct potential hazards. ##### HEALTH & SAFETY TRAINING * Lab personnel must receive: * General safety training, which includes General Safety & Emergency Preparedness (EHS-4200) and Chemical Safety for Laboratories (EHS-1900). * Lab-specific training, which includes reviewing the hazards of HF, safety precautions, and emergency procedures. The SOP, Safety Data Sheet (SDS), and this fact sheet can be used for such training. * Keep training records for at least one year. ### Emergency procedures * Post SU [Hydrofluoric Acid (HF) Exposure Response Instructions](https://ehs.stanford.edu/safety-store/hydrofluoric-acid-hf-exposure-response-poster) in labs that store or use HF solutions or gas. * Ensure 2.5% calcium gluconate gel (intended for dermal exposures) is available in laboratory and stored at the safety shower. * Ensure gel has an effective shelf life of at least one year. * Implement a system to refresh your supply of gel before the expiration date. * Gel can be purchased through many lab safety supply vendors, including Stanford Express. ##### SPILLS For spills occurring outside of a fume hood, evacuate the area. Immediately call EH&S at (650) 725-9999 (286 from School of Medicine phones) to report an HF spill that is health threatening, is outside of the fume hood, is greater than 30 mls, or will take longer than 15 minutes to clean up. Do not use spill kits with kitty litter or sand because HF reacts with silica to produce silicon tetrafluoride, a toxic gas. See SU’s [Laboratory Chemical Safety Toolkit](https://ehs.stanford.edu/forms-tools/laboratory-chemical-safety-toolkit) for additional guidance on spills. ##### SKIN EXPOSURE 1. Immediately flush affected area with water for 5 minutes under emergency shower station. Remove all contaminated clothing while flushing with water. 2. After flushing, apply calcium gluconate to burn site with clean, gloved hand. Continue massaging gel into the burned area of skin every 15 minutes and massaged continuously until pain disappears. 3. Immediately seek emergency medical assistance Call: * 9-911 from SU phones * 911 from non-SU phones * 286 from School of Medicine phones Or, take victim to seek medical evaluation at Stanford Hospital Emergency Room. ##### INHALATION For emergency medical assistance, call: * 9-911 from SU phones * 911 from non-SU phones * 286 from School of Medicine phones Or, take victim to seek medical evaluation at Stanford Hospital Emergency Room. ##### EYE EXPOSURE 1. Immediately flush eyes with water for at least 15 minutes under emergency eyewash. If only one eye is affected, be careful not to flush contaminated water into the other eye. 2. For emergency medical assistance, call: * 9-911 from SU phones * 911 from non-SU phones * 286 from School of Medicine phones Or, take victim to seek medical evaluation at Stanford Hospital Emergency Room. If possible, provide continuous irrigation during transport. ##### INGESTION 1. Rinse mouth with cold water. Do not induce vomiting. 2. If the victim is conscious, have them drink lots of water to dilute the acid. 3. For emergency medical assistance, call: * 9-911 from SU phones * 911 from non-SU phones * 286 from School of Medicine phones Or, take victim to seek medical evaluation at Stanford Hospital Emergency Room. ### Reference 1 <https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7108671/> 2 <https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/773304-overview> 3 [“Medical Management Guidelines for Hydrogen Fluoride”. Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry.](https://www.atsdr.cdc.gov/mmg/mmg.asp?id=1142&tid=250)",,,,,,,基于公开网页资料自动抽取，导入知识库前请人工复核。提取通道=direct；quality_score=0.7061。,Information on Hydrofluoric Acid | https://ehs.stanford.edu/reference/information-hydrofluoric-acid,网页,Information on Hydrofluoric Acid,Stanford Environmental Health & Safety,,2014/06/27,https://ehs.stanford.edu/reference/information-hydrofluoric-acid,2026-04-27,,draft,hydrofluoric acid;HF;calcium gluconate;SOP;PPE,en
WEBX-005-001,Information on Pyrophoric Compounds,化学,危化品管理,化学,5,危化品;火灾,What risk controls are required for pyrophoric and air-sensitive compounds in research labs,What risk controls are required for pyrophoric and air-sensitive compounds in research labs有哪些要点？,"[__Download](https://ehs.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/Pyrophoric-Materials-Fact-Sheet.pdf) [__Print](javascript:window.print\(\)) [](https://www.addtoany.com/add_to/email?linkurl=https%3A%2F%2Fehs.stanford.edu%2Freference%2Finformation-on-pyrophoric-compounds&linkname=Information%20on%20Pyrophoric%20Compounds ""Email"")[](https://www.addtoany.com/add_to/copy_link?linkurl=https%3A%2F%2Fehs.stanford.edu%2Freference%2Finformation-on-pyrophoric-compounds&linkname=Information%20on%20Pyrophoric%20Compounds ""Copy Link"") Pyrophoric materials have the potential to spontaneously ignite upon exposure to the oxygen in air, and in many cases are also water reactive. Exposure to air or moisture can cause them to generate heat and/or fire, as well as flammable and/or corrosive byproducts, by violent decompositions. Pyrophoric materials can take the form of solids, liquids, or gasses. In a chemistry research setting, they are often used in a flammable solvent solution, increasing the fire risk. Examples of pyrophoric materials include, but are not limited to: * Organolithium reagents (e.g., alkyl and aryl lithiums, lithium acetylide, lithium amide) * Organohalogen or Grignard reagents (e.g., RMgX, where R=alkyl and X=halogen) * Alkyl metal bases (e.g., butyllithiums, trimethylaluminum, triethylboron) * Metal hydrides (e.g., sodium hydride, germane, lithium aluminum hydride) * Finely divided metals (e.g., Raney nickel, aluminum powder, zinc dust) * Used [hydrogenation](/reference/hydrogenation-information-sheet) catalysts (e.g., palladium on carbon) * Metal carbonyls (e.g., nickel carbonyl, iron pentacarbonyl) * Organozincs (e.g., diethylzinc) * Aluminum alkyls (e.g., trimethylaluminum) * Silicon halides (e.g., dichloromethylsilane) * Red and white phosphorus **The organolithium reagent _tert_ -butyllithium (_t_ -BuLi) is often the greatest cause for concern due to its extreme reactivity.** In December 2008, a laboratory incident involving the use of t-BuLi resulted in severe burns and the subsequent death of a young researcher at UCLA. This chemical is highly hazardous and requires special training to handle properly; it is designated as a Restricted Chemical in the SU [Chemical Hygiene Plan](/manual/chemical-hygiene-plan) (Section 5.1), which requires PI approval prior to use. Section 5.2 of the Chemical Hygiene Plan lists methods for granting prior approval. For further information on the UCLA incident, visit [this page](https://cen.acs.org/articles/87/i31/Learning-UCLA.html). ### What are the hazards? Before beginning work, researchers must familiarize themselves with the specific hazards and toxicity of compounds. Performing a Risk Assessment is one way of completing this process. General information about risk assessments, with a link to a template, and training can be found on the Stanford EH&S website. A Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) is another method to complete a risk assessment. General hazards for pyrophoric materials may include: * Ignition on contact with air and/or water, potentially causing fires or explosions. * Some are toxic and may come dissolved or immersed in a flammable solvent. * Other common hazards include corrosivity, toxicity, teratogenicity, peroxide formation, or damage to the liver, kidneys, and central nervous system. Specific hazard information may be found using manufacturer labels and safety data sheets (SDS) and/or from the following sources: * [Bretherick’s Guide to Reactive Chemical Hazards](https://searchworks.stanford.edu/view/4301843) * [e-EROS (Encyclopedia of Reagents for Organic Synthesis)](https://searchworks.stanford.edu/view/5522540) * [Sittig’s Handbook of Bretherick’s handbook of reactive chemical hazards in SearchWorks catalogToxic and Hazardous Chemicals and Carcinogens](https://searchworks.stanford.edu/view/4301843) * [Wiley Guide to Chemical Incompatibilities](https://searchworks.stanford.edu/view/12156244) * [NIOSH Pocket Guide](https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/npg/) * [The Synthetic Organic Chemist’s Companion](https://searchworks.stanford.edu/view/7542368) * [Handbook of Synthetic Organic Chemistry](https://searchworks.stanford.edu/view/11847701) * [CAMEO Chemicals](https://cameochemicals.noaa.gov/) * [Stanford Chemical Safety Database](https://chemtracker.stanford.edu/CTv1/servlet/chemsafe.lookup) * [DeepResearch (Stanford Library custom chemical safety search engine)](https://searchworks.stanford.edu/view/9350224) ### How do I store this? The storage of unused reagents is a key aspect of working safely with pyrophoric materials. Researchers may utilize the [Stanford Compatible Storage Group Chemical Classification System](/forms-tools/stanford-storage-groups) to determine chemical compatibilities (pyrophoric materials are Storage Group B). To find the appropriate storage group for a given chemical, check your lab’s [online chemical inventory](https://ctprdweb.stanford.edu/?account=stanford&state=login) or search using Stanford’s [Chemical Safety Database](https://chemtracker.stanford.edu/CTv1/servlet/chemsafe.lookup). * Store as directed by the manufacturer. * Keep pyrophoric chemicals segregated from flammable/combustible materials or other incompatible chemicals. * Avoid storage areas near to heat/flames, oxidizers, and water sources. * Minimize quantities of pyrophoric material stored in the laboratory. * Date all containers upon receipt and periodically check the condition of the container and material. * Containers carrying pyrophoric materials must be clearly labeled with the correct chemical name and hazard warning. * Store pyrophoric materials in a secondary container of sufficient size, with only compatible materials in the secondary. ### How can I work safely? As part of the scientific process, researchers and PIs must continually consider how to minimize or eliminate exposure to workplace hazards. The most widely accepted system for controlling exposure to hazards is the [Hierarchy of Controls](https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/topics/hierarchy/default.html) from the National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health (NIOSH). In this system, researchers consider different methods to manage/reduce exposure, in order of effectiveness. (1) Elimination: Remove the pyrophoric reagent from the process. This is the most effective risk reduction method and requires creativity. * Develop alternative synthetic routes in which pyrophoric or highly reactive compounds are not required to accomplish your procedure successfully. * Consider changing your target end product if other products may be equally effective for the application and do not require the use of pyrophoric reagents. (2) Substitution: Use a less hazardous reagent. * Resources such as [March’s advanced organic chemistry](https://searchworks.stanford.edu/view/6844528) can be used to explore alternate reagents. * For example, the following [trends](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/9780470504437.app1) apply to estimate the pyrophoricity of metal alkyls. **Change in Property** | **Effect on Pyrophoricity** ---|--- Metal Content ↑ | Pyrophoricity ↑ Oxygen content ↑ | Pyrophoricity ↓ Solvent vapor pressure ↑ (or boiling point ↓) | Pyrophoricity ↑ Temperature ↑ | Pyrophoricity ↑ * Use less electronegative (higher atomic number) halogen components in organohalogen reagents (e.g., Grignard reagents) to reduce bond polarity, which also reduces reactivity and hazard. * Reactivity: C-F > C-Cl > C-Br >> C-I. * Use organolithium reagents with less bulky alkyl groups to reduce reactivity. * _n_ -butyllithium is significantly more stable, less reactive, and therefore less hazardous, than _tert_ -butyllithium, although they are both pyrophoric. * Reactivity: _n_ -BuLi < _sec_ -BuLi < _t_ -BuLi. This relationship holds for all organometal compounds. * Similarly, _n_ -hexyllithium should be considered an alternative to _n_ -butyllithium due to its lower tendency to ignite when compared to _n_ -butyllithym. (3) Engineering controls: Isolate people from the hazard. * Perform work in a glove box or glove bag under inert atmosphere (e.g., nitrogen, argon) to reduce exposure to oxygen and/or water. * Work in a fume hood, over a spill tray, using the appropriate sash height and working distance. For more information on proper fume hood use, please see the Laboratory [Ventilation Management Program](/manual/laboratory-ventilation-management-program), Section 9.1.1. * Use techniques that prevent the material from contacting air, such as vacuum manifold techniques (i.e., [Schlenk technique](https://schlenklinesurvivalguide.com/schlenk-line-safety/)) if working in a fume hood. * Store pyrophoric gases in ventilated toxic gas cabinets. * Utilize blast shields if there is a potential explosion hazard (these may be borrowed from the [Stanford EH&S Safety Store](/safety-store/blast-shield)). (4) Administrative controls: Use safe work practices. * Require proper training and practice before handling pyrophoric materials. * Never leave potentially hazardous experiments unattended; if this is unavoidable, post signage identifying the contents, hazards, and your contact information. * [Do not work alone](/topic/lab-safety/working-alone-guidance) while handling pyrophoric materials, use an in-person buddy who is knowledgeable of the hazards and able to appropriately respond to an emergency. * Basic training requirements are the same as those identified in Section 10 of the [Stanford University Chemical Hygiene Plan](/manual/chemical-hygiene-plan). * Specific training requirements include: * Fire Extinguisher Use in Labs (EHS-3700) * Laboratory-specific training, such as review of SDS for chemicals involved in process/experiment and review of the standard operating procedure (SOP) * PI Approval of each new user * Training can be documented on a PI-approved SOP * Develop material- and lab-specific safe operating procedures (SOPs). Example considerations are listed below. * For reference, see [Stanford’s General Use SOP for Highly Reactive/Unstable Materials](/wp-content/uploads/sops/Highly Reactive and Unstable Materials.pdf). * EH&S also provides a specific [_tert_ -butyllithium SOP template](/wp-content/uploads/sops/21-171-tBuLi-SOP-template.docx). While the techniques in this template are only necessary for _tert_ -butyllithium, they are good practices for working with any pyrophoric material. * Use and purchase materials in the smallest quantities necessary and design experiments on the smallest scale or lowest concentration possible. The PI should set quantity limits for pyrophorics in their labs. * Larger volumes of pyrophoric liquids (> 20 mL) should be transferred by cannula directly into the reaction flask or a calibrated addition funnel. * If using a syringe, purchase a Luer lock with a long reusable needle and hard plastic or glass plunger, and only fill the syringe to half of its working volume to avoid accidental exposure. * Never reuse a syringe for more than one transfer, as this can lead to [clogging of the barrel](https://cen.acs.org/articles/87/i31/Learning-UCLA.html) * Do not transfer more than 20 mL of pyrophoric liquids by syringe. * If the original manufacturer’s container that has an air-sensitive seal or cap, use a non-coring needle no larger than 16-gauge and create a new puncture hole each time, which avoids enlarging existing holes. * Note: Sigma-Aldrich warns that the [SureSeal](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/technical-documents/technical-article/chemistry-and-synthesis/organic-reaction-toolbox/sureseal) cap (and similar caps from other suppliers) may lose integrity over multiple uses. If the bottle will be used multiple times and stored, they recommend transferring the contents to Schlenkware or using an [Oxford Valve Cap](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/aldrich/z406260) or [similar](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/product/aldrich/z407186) covering the SureSeal. See [AL-134](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/deepweb/assets/sigmaaldrich/marketing/global/documents/685/583/al_techbull_al134.pdf) and [AL-195](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/deepweb/assets/sigmaaldrich/product/documents/409/105/al_techbull__al195.pdf). * Dry all glassware, syringes, needles, atmosphere, solvents, and other reagents when working with water-reactive compounds. * Glassware and metal syringes can be dried by flaming under vacuum or in an [oven](/forms-tools/drying-oven-information-sheet) overnight. Allow the flask to cool in a dry atmosphere, such as a Schlenk line or in a desiccator. * [Compatible](/reference/n-butyllithium-lessons-learned) plastic syringes can be dried by storing in a desiccator with the plunger removed. * Solvents can be dried using a solvent system or by adding dried molecular sieves to a fresh bottle. Please note that solvent stills are discouraged due to excessive quantities of flammable solvents, water reactive drying agents (e.g., sodium metal) in combination with heat and a nearby water-cooled condenser. * Maintain good housekeeping practices. * Remove clutter from your work area. * Keep all combustible and flammable materials away from pyrophoric materials. * Follow manufacturer’s recommendations for use and storage. * Dispose of old bottles with excessive precipitates or unreliable concentrations. (5) PPE: Protect the worker with personal protective equipment as a last line of defense. * Always wear a fire resistant (FR) lab coat with tight cuffs. * Wear protective eyewear, such as fully enclosed safety goggles or face shield with safety glasses or goggles. * Wear [appropriate gloves](/topic/lab-safety/personal-protective-equipment#lab-gloves), such as flame-resistant Nomex gloves under nitrile gloves. * Tie back hair to avoid catching it on fire, exposing it to chemicals, or having it interfere with your vision. * Wear long pants (or equivalent) and closed-toe shoes that cover the whole foot. * Avoid loose sleeves that may get caught or knock something over. * Wear natural fabrics like cotton; avoid synthetic fibers, which can melt when ignited. ### How do I dispose of this? Disposal is often the final step in ensuring that a researcher and EH&S Environmental Protection staff safely handle pyrophoric materials. Improper disposal can lead to fires or other mishaps, injuries, or lasting effects on the environment. General guidelines are listed below. However, please reach out to the Stanford [Environmental Protection](/about-us/environmental-protection) team for any questions about waste disposal. * Handling and disposal should be done in accordance with the lab protocol approved by the Principal Investigator (PI). * Reactions should be quenched before disposal. Ensure proper training before quenching procedures are attempted. * Bulk materials, like excess reagent in the original manufacturer’s container, should not be quenched prior to disposal, they can be tagged with a waste tag at [wastetag.stanford.edu](https://wastetag.stanford.edu/). * Ensure proper containment before removal of a potentially pyrophoric material from a glove box or other inert atmosphere (e.g., original manufacturer’s container or properly sealed vessel). * Information about waste disposal procedures can be found on the Stanford EH&S website [here](/topic/waste-disposal). ### Reference * Aldrich Technical Bulletin [AL-164](https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/deepweb/assets/sigmaaldrich/marketing/global/documents/255/911/al_techbull_al164.pdf) * e-Eros Encyclopedia of Reagents for Organic Synthesis: [_t_](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/047084289X.rb396.pub2)[-Butyllithium](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/047084289X.rb396.pub2) * PNNL [Technical Report](https://www.pnnl.gov/main/publications/external/technical_reports/PNNL-18668.pdf?_ga=2.252236041.1019597946.1639158181-94362325.1633555399) on Handling Pyrophoric Reagents * LBL [Chemical Hygiene and Safety Plan](https://sites.google.com/lbl.gov/chsp-ch45/home) * Lessons learned * _C &EN_ [Lessons Of The Tragedy At UCLA](https://cen.acs.org/articles/87/i40/Lessons-Tragedy-UCLA.html) * Stanford [_n_ -Butyllithium](/reference/n-butyllithium-lessons-learned) Lessons Learned * UC Davis [Pyrophoric Metal Fire](https://safetyservices.ucdavis.edu/lessons-learned/lesson-learned-pyrophoric-metal-fire) Lessons Learned * UCI [Pyrophoric Materials Cause Chemical Fume Hood Fire](https://ehs.uci.edu/safety/pdfs/i_fume_hood_fire.pdf) Lessons Learned * BNL [Silane Safety/Lessons Learned and Accident Prevention](https://www.bnl.gov/pv/files/pdf/IEEE_May2008_Silane_Tutorial.pdf) * Safety Partners [Titanium: Beautiful but Dangerous](https://www.safetypartnersinc.com/incidents-accidents-and-near-misses-titanium-beautiful-but-dangerous/) Incidents, Accidents, and Near Misses * Resources from other universities * Princeton [Pyrophoric Materials](https://ehs.princeton.edu/laboratory-research/chemical-safety/reactive-materials/pyrophoric-materials) * UIUC [Handling Pyrophoric and other Air/Water Reactive Materials](https://drs.illinois.edu/Page/SafetyLibrary/PyrophoricMaterials) * UMN [Pyrophoric Chemicals Guide](https://dehs.umn.edu/sites/dehs.umn.edu/files/fs_pyrophoric_chemicals_guide.pdf) * UCSB [Safe Use of Pyrophoric/ Water Reactive Reagents](https://www.ehs.ucsb.edu/files/docs/ls/factsheets/Pyrophoric_FS37.pdf) Fact Sheet * CMich [Pyrophoric & Water-Reactive Chemicals](https://www.cmich.edu/office_provost/ORGS/Lab_Safety/Documents/Appendix%20Q%20-%20Pyrophoric%20and%20Water-Reactive%20Chemicals.pdf) Appendix * CMU [Pyrophoric Handling](https://www.cmu.edu/ehs/Guidelines/pyrophorichandlingprocedure.pdf) Procedure",,,,,,,基于公开网页资料自动抽取，导入知识库前请人工复核。提取通道=direct；quality_score=0.7254。,Information on Pyrophoric Compounds | https://ehs.stanford.edu/reference/working-air-sensitive-or-highly-reactive-compounds,网页,Information on Pyrophoric Compounds,Stanford Environmental Health & Safety,,,https://ehs.stanford.edu/reference/working-air-sensitive-or-highly-reactive-compounds,2026-04-27,,draft,pyrophoric;air sensitive;water reactive;SOP;fire safety,en
WEBX-007-001,5.11 Peroxide Forming Chemicals,化学,危化品管理,化学,5,危化品;火灾;储存,How should peroxide-forming chemicals be dated tested stored and disposed,How should peroxide-forming chemicals be dated tested stored and disposed有哪些要点？,"### Characteristics Over a period of time, these chemicals can form peroxides that may explode when the cap is removed or when they are concentrated during laboratory activities. It is important to note on the container the date the chemical arrived in the laboratory, when it was opened, when it should be tested for peroxide concentration, and when it should be discarded. Dispose of the chemical before the discard date indicated on the container or follow the guidelines below. **Dispose of within 24 Hours:** * acrylic acid (uninhibited) * butadiene (uninhibited) **Test or Dispose of within 3 Months:** * butadiene (inhibited) * chloroprene * divinylacetylene * isopropyl ether **Test or Dispose of within 12 Months:** * acetaldehyde * acrolein * benzyl ether * 2-butanol * cyclohexanol * diethyl ether * ethyl vinyl ether * 2-hexanol * 3-methyl-1-butanol * tetrahydrofuran ### Storage and Disposal of Peroxide Forming Chemicals By the expiration date, the owner/user should either dispose of the chemical or test it for peroxide content. Dispose of any chemicals found to have a peroxide concentration greater than or equal to 100 parts per million. (Call EH&S for assistance.) Materials that have lasted beyond the recommended shelf life but have been tested and show no detectable peroxides, or whose peroxide concentrations are less than 100 ppm, may be retained but should be tested at frequent intervals. Test all peroxide forming chemicals prior to distillation, regardless of age. Important note: Never test containers of unknown age or origin. Old bottles are likely to contain concentrated peroxides, and peroxides may have crystallized in the cap threads, which can present a serious hazard when the bottle is opened for testing. Contact [ucbcho@berkeley.edu](mailto:ucbcho@berkeley.edu) for help with managing older containers and for additional guidelines on the safe storage and handling of peroxide forming chemicals. ### Storage Precautions for Peroxide Forming Chemicals * Identify all peroxide forming chemicals in your inventory. * Write the opening date and discard date on the containers of chemicals that may degrade to become potentially explosive. * Store in airtight containers in a dark, cool, and dry place. * Discard or test peroxide forming chemicals once expired. Contact EH&S for disposal information. * Inspect peroxide forming chemicals often for evidence of contamination, degradation, or any change from normal physical or chemical characteristics. Contact EH&S immediately if you suspect a material shows signs of stratification, discoloration or crystal growth. Post warning signs so others do not handle or disturb the material. * Note: Most explosions occur while purifying or distilling mixtures. Therefore, use extreme caution before concentrating or purifying any mixture that may contain an explosive chemical . * For more extensive information regarding potentially explosive chemicals, please see the “[Guidelines for Explosive and Potentially Explosive Chemicals Safe Storage and Handling](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/publications/guidelines-explosive-and-potentially-explosive-chemicals-safe-storage-and-handling)”",,,,,,,基于公开网页资料自动抽取，导入知识库前请人工复核。提取通道=direct；quality_score=0.6136。,5.11 Peroxide Forming Chemicals | https://ehs.berkeley.edu/safe-storage-hazardous-chemicals/511-peroxide-forming-chemicals,网页,5.11 Peroxide Forming Chemicals,UC Berkeley EH&S,,,https://ehs.berkeley.edu/safe-storage-hazardous-chemicals/511-peroxide-forming-chemicals,2026-04-27,,draft,peroxide forming chemicals;expiration;testing;chemical storage,en
WEBX-009-001,5.6 Compressed Gases,设备安全,气瓶管理,通用,4,高压气体;高压;易燃气体;剧毒,What storage and leak-response precautions apply to compressed gas cylinders in labs,What storage and leak-response precautions apply to compressed gas cylinders in labs有哪些要点？,"### Characteristics Compressed gas cylinders are pressurized vessels designed to store gases at high pressure. Because of this internal pressure, damage to a cylinder, such as dropping or tipping it over, can result in sudden, uncontrolled release of contents and forceful movement of the cylinder. In addition to the physical hazards associated with pressurization, further risks may arise depending on the properties of the gas, including toxicity, flammability, corrosivity, or chemical reactivity. ### Laboratory Chemicals * argon * butane * carbon monoxide * chlorine * ethylene * hydrogen * methane * nitrogen ### Non-Laboratory Chemicals * acetylene * compressed air * oxygen ### Storage Precautions for Compressed Gases * Segregate incompatible gases as you would other incompatible chemicals. Flammable and oxidizing gases should be separated by a minimum of 20 feet or a non-combustible barrier. * Limit the quantity of compressed gas cylinders on site to what will be used within a reasonable period of time. * Store cylinders upright. * Secure cylinders so they will not fall during an earthquake. * An acceptable means includes using two non-combustible restraints, such as chains, one restraint located approximately one-third of the cylinder length from the top, and the other restraint one-third from the bottom. * Keep cylinders away from heat and open flames. * Leave the valve protection cap on the cylinder unless it is in use. * Never store cylinders in walk-in freezers. The confined space with no ventilation poses a potential hazard. * If you suspect that a cylinder is leaking, do not attempt to sniff the leak out. Apply a soap solution to the cylinder and locate the leak by noting where the bubbles appear. Toxic gases, highly toxic gases, and pyrophoric gases must be managed in accordance with the campus [Toxic, Corrosive and Pyrophoric (TCP)Gas Program](https://ehs.berkeley.edu/tcp-gas-program) requirements. Contact [toxicgas@berkeley.edu](mailto:toxicgas@berkeley.edu) for details about ventilation and quantity limitations.",,,,,,,基于公开网页资料自动抽取，导入知识库前请人工复核。提取通道=direct；quality_score=0.4549。,5.6 Compressed Gases | https://ehs.berkeley.edu/safe-storage-hazardous-chemicals/56-compressed-gases,网页,5.6 Compressed Gases,UC Berkeley EH&S,,,https://ehs.berkeley.edu/safe-storage-hazardous-chemicals/56-compressed-gases,2026-04-27,,draft,compressed gas;cylinder storage;leak check;segregation,en
WEBX-010-001,Compressed Gas Cylinder Safety,设备安全,气瓶管理,通用,4,高压气体;高压;低温,How should laboratories secure and manage compressed gas cylinders,How should laboratories secure and manage compressed gas cylinders有哪些要点？,"In addition to the following general guidelines, refer to the Standard Operating Procedure (SOP): [EHS-0001 Compressed Gases](https://ehs.mit.edu/sops/?title=Compressed+Gases&key_term=) (certificate required). The SOP includes information about different hazard classes, transporting within campus, etc. For Cryogenics please refer to [Cryogen Safety](https://ehs.mit.edu/chemical-safety-program/chemicals/) under Chemical – Chemicals: Best Practices and Guidance for Specific Chemicals, [Cryogen Safety web course](http://web.mit.edu/training/course.html?course=EHS00466w&sys=PS1) in the MIT Atlas Learning Center, and the [Cryogenic Liquids SOP](https://ehs.mit.edu/sops/?title=Cryogenic+Liquids&key_term=) (certificate required). ## Guidelines for Securing Cylinders Expand All Securing Restrain compressed gas cylinders securely with a chain, belt, or stand at all times to prevent them from falling over. If a cylinder falls, it may shear off its valve, and the escaping high-pressure gas has been known to propel the cylinder like an unguided rocket. It can smash through masonry walls, go long distances and ricochet. Secure the cylinder above its center of gravity (~2/3 up the cylinder). If the chain or belt is too low or too high, it will not hold the cylinder if it starts to fall. The chain should not be around the neck of the cylinder since the cylinder could slide under the chain. The chain should not hang below the midpoint of a cylinder since it could topple over the chain. Protect the cylinder valve with a securely attached valve cap whenever a cylinder is being moved, and at all other times unless a regulator is attached to the cylinder. Store flammable gases separately from oxygen and combustible materials by at least 20 feet. Highly toxic (health rating 4), pyrophoric gases, nitrous oxide, and other DHS gases should be secured from unauthorized access. Cylinder Wall Brackets With Straps/ Belts Create a service request in Atlas for the Department of Facilities (DOF) to obtain and mount standard wall brackets. These need to be securely attached to hold the weight of the cylinders. Or you can order wall brackets from VWR or Linde and then create a service request in Atlas for DOF to mount your brackets. Wall brackets are strong enough to hold two cylinders strapped together. However, when the strap is taken off to move one cylinder, then both could fall over. Inspect Straps and Replace Frayed/Ripped Straps Follow the steps in the attached Cylinder Strap Inspection Tips. If necessary, order a replacement from any of the vendors listed on the attached comparison of cylinder strap/holder. Polyester or nylon cylinder straps will resist fraying better than the older straps that are made out of fabric. If you find a frayed or ripped strap, replace it with a spare strap that is in good condition or use a chain.",,,,,,,基于公开网页资料自动抽取，导入知识库前请人工复核。提取通道=direct；quality_score=0.593。,Compressed Gas Cylinder Safety | https://ehs.mit.edu/chemical-safety-program/compressed-gas-cylinder-safety/,网页,Compressed Gas Cylinder Safety,MIT EHS,,,https://ehs.mit.edu/chemical-safety-program/compressed-gas-cylinder-safety/,2026-04-27,,draft,compressed gas;cylinder restraints;transport;training,en
